{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"6d482d5a11a34740eb1202233c7aa984d1782d22"},"timestamp":1545078009,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzOTE4NzQ3ODM=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3092","origin":"github"},"title":"Progress is printed to stderr even if not attached to a TTY","message":"#### What is the problem?\n\nWhen not attached to a TTY, datalad still attempts to print a progress bar and other diagnostic information to stderr, which acts as noise when trying to programmatically do something with its output in a script such as in a unix pipeline.\n\n#### What steps will reproduce the problem?\n\n`datalad create 2\u003e\u00261 | cat \u003e ../x` and open file `x` with a text editor. Note the number of `\\r` characters output as well as progress information which is meaningless when not attached to an interactive terminal. (You can replace `datalad create` with your favourite subcommand.)\n\n#### What version of DataLad are you using (run `datalad --version`)? On what operating system (consider running `datalad wtf`)?\n\nI happened to be using 0.10.1 when I just tried, but given that there's no related issues that GitHub is finding I suspect nothing's been done to change it.\n\n#### Is there anything else that would be useful to know in this context?\n\nI'm just relaying a message from someone I heard complain about this and thought it would be worth noting. The specifics are about the progress bar, but the general complaint can be summarized as \"it should be easier to systematically parse the output when invoked in a script.\"","files":null}]}